<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # These Plants Are from Panama. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> These Plants Are from Panama.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Engage in questions and answers about familiar subjects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe and perform simple gardening tasks (e.g., 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.'). Can ask and answer questions about gardening when collaborating on a project (e.g., 'How do you plant the tomatoes?' 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.'). Can ask and answer simple questions about their preferences related to gardening (e.g., 'Do you like watering the plants?' 'Yes, I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various flashcards or realia related to gardening (e.g., a small watering can, a packet of seeds, a small plant, soil in a cup). The teacher points to each item and asks, "What is this?" or "Do you like (tomatoes)?". Students respond chorally or individually. The teacher also shows pictures of fruits and vegetables (e.g., tomatoes) and asks "Are these from Panama?" to connect to the theme.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models simple gardening actions with gestures while speaking. "First, I <b>dig</b> a hole (teacher mimes digging). Then, I <b>plant</b> the <b>seed</b> (teacher mimes planting). I <b>water</b> the <b>plant</b> (teacher mimes watering with a watering can). I water the plants <b>every day</b>. They <b>grow</b> quickly." The teacher also models asking questions: "How do you plant a seed?" and "Do you like watering the plants?" The teacher specifically pronounces /t/ in 'tomatoes' and /g/ in 'grow' clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of digging) "Do I use a <b>watering can</b> or my hands to <b>dig</b> a hole?" (Expected answer: "Hands" or "My hands")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of a <b>seed</b>) "Do I <b>plant</b> a big <b>plant</b> or a small <b>seed</b>?" (Expected answer: "A small seed")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two friends, Carlos and María, about planting tomatoes and flowers in their garden. The dialogue is presented on a large chart or projector screen.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b><br>
                Carlos: "Hi María! What are you doing?"<br>
                María: "I'm in the <b>garden</b>. I <b>water</b> the <b>plants every day</b>.<br>
                Carlos: "Oh, these are nice <b>flowers</b>! And <b>these</b> are <b>tomatoes</b>, right?"<br>
                María: "Yes, <b>this</b> is a <b>tomato plant</b>. I <b>plant</b> the <b>seeds</b> in the <b>soil</b>."<br>
                Carlos: "How do you <b>plant</b> the <b>tomatoes</b>?"<br>
                María: "<b>First</b>, you <b>dig</b> a hole. <b>Second</b>, you put the <b>seed</b>. Then, you cover it with <b>soil</b> and <b>water</b> it with the <b>watering can</b>. I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging."<br>
                Carlos: "Do the <b>plants need sunlight</b>?"<br>
                María: "Yes, they <b>need sunlight</b> to <b>grow</b> and <b>bloom</b>. They <b>grow quickly</b>!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in questions and answers about familiar subjects.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Carlos and María talking about?" (Expected: Gardening / Plants / Tomatoes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads statements, students say True or False.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"María waters the plants <b>every day</b>." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Carlos likes <b>digging</b>." (False - María doesn't like digging)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"<b>These</b> are <b>tomato plants</b>." (True, pointing to a picture if available)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Present simple + 's', Demonstratives (this, these), Question forms (How do you...?, Do you like...?), Vocabulary (garden, plant, tomato, seed, soil, watering can, grow, water, dig, need, every day, sometimes, always, first, second, like, don't like, and, but). Pronunciation practice for /t/ and /g/ sounds. Pragmatic functions: Describing steps, routines, preferences. Sociolinguistic: Describing instructions with conjunctions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Guided Sentence Completion (Oral Cloze).</b> The teacher reads sentences aloud, pausing for students to fill in the missing word orally. Students can complete chorally or individually.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"First, you _______ a hole." (dig)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Then, you _______ the seed." (plant)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I _______ the plants every day." (water)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"María _______ the plants every day." (waters)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"_______ is a tomato plant." (This) / "_______ are flowers." (These)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Substitution Drill (Pair Work).</b> Students work in pairs with sentence frames provided by the teacher on the board.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame 1: "I like [gardening task], but I don't like [other gardening task]." (e.g., watering the plants, digging, planting seeds). Partner A says a preference, Partner B repeats and substitutes.
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "I like watering the plants."</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging."</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame 2: "How often do you [gardening activity]?" (e.g., water the plants, dig the soil). Partner A asks, Partner B answers using adverbs of frequency.
                                <ul>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: "How often do you water the plants?"</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: "I water the plants <b>every day</b>." (or <b>sometimes</b>, <b>always</b>)</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Describe the Steps (Pair Interaction).</b> Students are given picture cards depicting different gardening steps (digging, planting a seed, watering, growing, harvesting). In pairs, students take turns picking a card and describing the step using sequence words and conjunctions.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A: (Picks "planting a seed" card) "<b>First</b>, you <b>dig</b> a hole, <b>and</b> then you <b>put</b> the <b>seed</b>."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B: (Picks "watering" card) "You <b>add rainwater</b> with a <b>hose</b>, <b>and</b> the <b>plant needs sunlight</b> to <b>grow</b>."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Gardening Expert Role-Play."</b> Students work in pairs. One student plays a "New Gardener" who wants to plant something from Panama (e.g., tomatoes, a specific flower), and the other student plays a "Gardening Expert" who knows how to do it. The New Gardener asks for instructions and expresses preferences, and the Expert provides clear, sequential instructions, and also shares preferences, using the target language. The role-play should incorporate question forms, present simple, demonstratives, sequence words, and expressions of preference.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher assigns roles and reminds students to use the vocabulary and grammar practiced.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">New Gardener initiates: "Hello! I want to plant <b>tomatoes</b>. How do you <b>plant</b> them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gardening Expert responds: "Okay. <b>First</b>, you <b>dig</b> a hole in the <b>soil</b>. Then, you <b>put</b> the <b>seed</b>, <b>and</b> you <b>cover</b> it. After that, you <b>water</b> it with the <b>watering can</b>. We water them <b>every day</b>, and they <b>grow quickly</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">New Gardener asks about care/preferences: "Do you like <b>watering</b> the <b>plants</b>?" or "What do the <b>plants need</b>?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Gardening Expert answers: "Yes, I like <b>watering</b> the <b>plants</b>, <b>but</b> I don't like <b>digging</b>. <b>These</b> plants <b>need sunlight</b>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pairs perform their role-play for each other. Some pairs may present to the class.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play to note student's use of target language. Peer assessment during the role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Gardening Expert Role-Play" from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Feedback):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students pronounce /t/ (tomatoes) and /g/ (grow) clearly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use at least 5 words related to gardening (e.g., <b>plant, seed, soil, water, dig, watering can, tomato</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target grammar:</b> Students use present simple + 's' (e.g., 'María waters'), demonstratives (<b>this/these</b>), and question forms (<b>How do you...?, Do you like...?</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of communication:</b> Instructions are clear and easy to follow. Answers are relevant.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Students listen to their partner and respond appropriately.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences or answer two questions before leaving the class.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask a friend: 'How do you _______ a _______?'" (e.g., plant a seed) (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can tell my friend two things I like or don't like about gardening." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases about the garden did you use today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for you when talking about helping in the garden?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The pair role-play activity worked well, and most students were able to ask and answer questions about gardening steps and preferences using sequence words and conjunctions. The use of flashcards for practicing sequencing was effective. Students were generally confident expressing "like/don't like". Pronunciation of /t/ in 'tomatoes' was good, but some students struggled with /g/ in 'grow'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments needed: Provide more explicit practice for the 's' ending in third-person present simple (e.g., 'he waters,' 'she digs'). Scaffold demonstratives (this/these) more clearly with visual cues during practice. More modeling and repetition for /g/ sound.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job speaking about our garden today! In our next lesson (Lesson 4), we will take the information we discussed about how to plant and care for our garden, and we will practice writing it down in simple sentences and instructions."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>